    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the Bronx during the 1970s, you could often see African American and Latino gangs asserting dominion over certain street corners and neighborhoods. The Crips, predominantly African American, often portrayed a tough, aggressive demeanor, their power fueled by territorial disputes and narcotics dealings. Conversely, the Bloods, also mainly African American, maintained a bravado presence, with internal family allegiances dictating their actions. White gangs also existed, embroiled in conflict with the primarily Black and Latino gangs, often led by rival narratives and fueled by racial tensions characteristic of the time.\n\nThe independent 'youth gangs' were a mix, with Hispanic and African American sub-cultures evident in groups such as the Latin Kings or Sores (South Bronx Representatives) that would valiantly uphold their names while occasionally clashing over turf and control, oftentimes steering clear of drug propagation to assert their dominance or reclaim territory.\n\nWhite suburban youth of the wealthy \"stockings,\" frequently portrayed as smug or elitist by lower-income view in media often found themselves in overt or covert conflicts with these gangs. Characterizing exactly which racial or ethnic demographics to feature largely depends on the narrative and the specific scenes in question, as many 'street kids' also transcended categor",
